<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molina de Aragón</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Molina de Aragón is a town on the Gallo river in the province of Guadalajara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, dominated by one of the largest castles in the country. A Muslim border lordship in the 11th century, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> around 1129 and became the semi-independent Lordship of Molina under the Lara family, with its own fuero. It passed to the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in the 1290s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/molina-de-aragon.jpg" alt="Molina de Aragón" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molina_de_Arag%C3%B3n,_Guadalajara,_Espa%C3%B1a,_2016-01-05,_DD_15.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
